      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 11:13 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2019-18-1233",
      "confidence": 0.99,
      "document_hash": "e3c3c1a79769bd20ef10a9b5e5a4ece028f1eede9b3ad41cda59b2bef260d1dd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e3c3c1a79769-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 7,
        "start": 7
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: Ms. Blatt, could you concentrate on the word \"equity\"? Do you think equity would sustain an award for innocent or good-faith infringement without a more culpable state of mind? Because there's a wide swath of behavior between truly innocent, truly good faith, and willful. There could be reckless. There could be callous disregard. Would equity consonance an award for negligence or good faith?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2019-18-1233",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e3c3c1a79769bd20ef10a9b5e5a4ece028f1eede9b3ad41cda59b2bef260d1dd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e3c3c1a79769-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: Well, how do you do this?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2019-18-1233",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e3c3c1a79769bd20ef10a9b5e5a4ece028f1eede9b3ad41cda59b2bef260d1dd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e3c3c1a79769-0001-0004",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 31,
        "start": 31
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "ORAL ARGUMENT OF NEAL K. KATYAL ON BEHALF OF THE RESPONDENTS"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Gorsuch",
      "case_id": "2019-18-1233",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e3c3c1a79769bd20ef10a9b5e5a4ece028f1eede9b3ad41cda59b2bef260d1dd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e3c3c1a79769-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GORSUCH: And why didn't you make an argument that we should, as a background principle, assume some sort of consistency with the common law when Congress was legislating?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Katyal",
      "case_id": "2019-18-1233",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e3c3c1a79769bd20ef10a9b5e5a4ece028f1eede9b3ad41cda59b2bef260d1dd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e3c3c1a79769-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. KATYAL: We certainly made exactly that argument citing Morissette in our brief for the idea, even if there weren't the price -- principles of equity, Congress acts against the backdrop of the common law and is deemed to interpret it."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2019-18-1233",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e3c3c1a79769bd20ef10a9b5e5a4ece028f1eede9b3ad41cda59b2bef260d1dd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e3c3c1a79769-0002-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: But -- but as Justice Sotomayor just pointed out, there wasn't -- there isn't in the cases a uniform agreement on what \"willful\" means."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2019-18-1233",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e3c3c1a79769bd20ef10a9b5e5a4ece028f1eede9b3ad41cda59b2bef260d1dd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e3c3c1a79769-0002-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 48,
        "start": 48
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: No, I'm not just -- treble, but up to a limit?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Katyal",
      "case_id": "2019-18-1233",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e3c3c1a79769bd20ef10a9b5e5a4ece028f1eede9b3ad41cda59b2bef260d1dd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e3c3c1a79769-0002-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 48,
        "start": 48
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. KATYAL: Absolutely. The statute -- this is what we say at page 54 of our brief allows treble damages for that --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kavanaugh",
      "case_id": "2019-18-1233",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e3c3c1a79769bd20ef10a9b5e5a4ece028f1eede9b3ad41cda59b2bef260d1dd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e3c3c1a79769-0002-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 51,
        "start": 51
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAVANAUGH: You -- you've mentioned a couple times whether it's worth the candle to not have a willfulness requirement, but is it worth the candle to exclude all reckless cases as Justice Breyer has stated --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2019-18-1233",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e3c3c1a79769bd20ef10a9b5e5a4ece028f1eede9b3ad41cda59b2bef260d1dd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e3c3c1a79769-0002-0007",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 53,
        "start": 53
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAGAN: May I ask two questions about that tradition? The first is you've said several times that Ms. Blatt has zero cases, and I believe Ms. Blatt said that she had three cases."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2019-18-1233",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e3c3c1a79769bd20ef10a9b5e5a4ece028f1eede9b3ad41cda59b2bef260d1dd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e3c3c1a79769-0002-0009",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 60,
        "start": 60
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: Mr. Katyal, could you explain the features of trademark that make it different from copyright and patent where as -- if I understand correctly, you can get profits without showing willfulness?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Lisa S. Blatt",
      "case_id": "2019-18-1233",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e3c3c1a79769bd20ef10a9b5e5a4ece028f1eede9b3ad41cda59b2bef260d1dd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e3c3c1a79769-0002-0008",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 62,
        "start": 62
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. BLATT: You may want to cut me off. So I -- I don't know what to say. I didn't go to a fancy law school, but I'm very confident in my representation of the case law. Mishawaka is a case by you guys and you said in there, in the dissent, it was an innocent infringer, the profits were awarded."
    },
